      "id": 1,
      "text": "Elk Mills grew around a 19th‑century water‑powered Baldwin Mill on Big Elk Creek, drawing workers and businesses.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/elk_mills_maryland"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The mid‑1800s railroad arrival spurred worker housing like Frame Row and Brick Row, plus Price's General Store and a two‑room schoolhouse.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/elk_mills_maryland"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Baldwin United Methodist Church, founded 1892, still serves the hamlet and ran monthly meals for about 70 families in 2011.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/elk_mills_maryland"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The community lies 3.5 miles northeast of Elkton and 45 miles northeast of Baltimore, keeping a rural feel while near urban centers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/elk_mills_maryland"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Big Elk Creek’s watershed drains 51.6 square miles and feeds the Elk River, supporting fertile alluvial soils for local farming.",
